**The missing operation is smaller than composite Frobenius.** For one fixed curve \(E\), it suffices to compute the scalar
\[
u_\ell(N)=\det(1-g_p)\det(1-g_q)\pmod\ell,
\]
where \(g_r\) is Frobenius on \(E[\ell]\) at \(r\). A uniform algorithm for this scalar at primes \(\ell=O(\log N)\) gives the exact point count by CRT. Constructing the two Frobenius actions would solve a harder intermediate problem and can already expose a factor. I found no native scalar algorithm. A preregistered test of four computable substitutes on 7,200 semiprimes found no signal beyond the public congruences. [Frobenius barrier](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nonsmooth_results.md:313) · [Count recovery](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/ecount_results.md:367)

## E1. Where the prime enters

Assume \(N=pq\) for distinct odd primes of good reduction. Write \(A_r=r+1-a_r=\#E(\mathbf F_r)\). The target is the **projective** count \(M=A_pA_q\).

| Method | Prime-field operation and what happens over \(\mathbb Z/N\) |
|---|---|
| **Schoof and SEA — known** | Schoof constructs a generic \(\ell\)-torsion point \(P\) and tests \(\pi^2P+[r]P=[t]\pi P\). Computing \(\pi P\) uses \(x^r\bmod(\psi_\ell,r)\) and the corresponding \(y\)-coordinate. SEA uses an eigenvalue on a Frobenius-stable isogeny kernel at Elkies primes, or Frobenius orbit lengths on cyclic subgroups at Atkin primes. The division and modular polynomials still exist modulo \(N\). The two fibers can have different traces, kernels and orbit lengths. Substituting \(N\) changes both the power map and the scalar \([r]\). [Schoof’s account](https://numdam.org/item/JTNB_1995__7_1_219_0/) |
| **Pila and Edixhoven–Couveignes — known** | Pila determines the characteristic polynomial of field Frobenius acting on the torsion of a fixed abelian variety. Edixhoven–Couveignes constructs a finite Galois representation, then identifies Frobenius at a **specified prime** to read its trace. Precomputing that representation leaves two unknown local classes at \(N\). Composite coefficients are assembled from a factored index. Their Remark 2.2.4 illustrates the distinction already for divisor sums. [Pila](https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/pila/Frobenius.pdf) · [Edixhoven–Couveignes et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0605244) · [Repo analysis](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nativemod_results.md:27) |
| **Kedlaya and Lauder deformation — known** | Kedlaya lifts \(x\mapsto x^r\) to characteristic zero and computes its action on cohomology to sufficient \(r\)-adic precision. Lauder transports a *known Frobenius matrix* from a base fiber using a differential equation and Frobenius pullback on the parameter. A known curve or characteristic-zero connection supplies neither the two initial local matrices nor the substitutions \(t\mapsto t^p,t^q\). [Kedlaya](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0105031) · [Lauder](https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/lauder/papers/dtczDec6.pdf) |
| **Satoh, AGM and Witt vectors — known** | Satoh lifts an ordinary curve together with its distinguished degree-\(r\) Frobenius isogeny and extracts the unit root. Mestre’s AGM exploits the characteristic-two isogeny chain. Generalized AGM methods likewise specify the residue characteristic and canonical kernel. Lifting the equation modulo \(N^k\) does not select these kernels. Big Witt vectors have power operations over arbitrary rings, but their index-\(N\) operation does not select the \(p\)-operation on one component and the \(q\)-operation on the other. [Satoh et al.](https://doi.org/10.1016/S1071-5797(02)00013-8) · [AGM and Frobenius lifts](https://arxiv.org/abs/0911.1883) · [Witt-vector analysis](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/conn_alg.md:141) |
| **Harvey average polynomial time — known** | An accumulating remainder tree evaluates prime-indexed recurrences for **all** good primes up to \(B\). The average cost per output is polynomial in \(\log B\). Reaching a hidden balanced prime by that batch requires \(B\) of order \(\sqrt N\). Using \(N\) as one remainder-tree modulus also supplies the wrong recurrence endpoint. [Harvey](https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2014/179-2/p07) |

**Proved in the repo.** The distinction between \(x^N\) and local Frobenius has a short witness. Take \(B=(\mathbb Z/N)[y]/(y^2-d)\) with Jacobi symbol \((d/N)=-1\). True componentwise Frobenius sends \(y\) to \(cy\), where \(c\) equals \(+1\) at one prime and \(-1\) at the other. Then \(\gcd(c-1,N)\) splits \(N\). On Schoof’s division algebra, \((N/\ell)=-1\) likewise forces different local fixed-space dimensions, detectable by elimination. Literal \(x^N\) is cheap but generally fails additivity. Its use in nested computations belongs to PRIM, whose deterministic fragments remain partly open. These operator results leave the scalar \(u_\ell\) open. [Prop FROB](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nonsmooth_results.md:313) · [PRIM](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/prim_results.md:24)

## E2. Reformulations that avoid constructing Frobenius

- **Pairings, isogenies and resultants — known obstruction.** The Weil pairing identifies the determinant character. Its public product here is \(pq\bmod\ell\). The desired scalar also involves both traces. A modular polynomial describes geometric neighbors, while selecting rational neighbors requires local splitting data. Even complete projective action can lose the answer: \(I\) and \(-I\) act identically on the four lines of \(E[3]\), but \(\det(1-g)\) is respectively \(0\) and \(1\bmod3\). Division-polynomial resultants are computable for explicit inputs. The open step is finding an input whose resultant yields the desired *product* from \(N\) alone. [Isogeny volcanoes](https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.5370) · [Torsion-data analysis](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md:325)
- **Lifting and higher dimension — known.** For good reduction, smooth lifting gives \(\#E(\mathbb Z/N^k)=N^{k-1}M\). Increasing \(k\) repeats the unknown. Prime-to-\(N\) torsion lifts uniquely across these nilpotent thickenings, preserving the missing local actions. A genus-\(g\) Jacobian replaces each quadratic characteristic polynomial by one of degree \(2g\), still evaluated at \(1\) in each fiber. Kummer coordinates accelerate arithmetic and identify \(P\) with \(-P\), which can discard precisely the sign needed above. Moving \(N\) into the **level** of \(X_0(N)\) gives genuine factoring-equivalent counts over known small fields, but the standard explicit models grow on the scale of \(N\). [Lifting identity](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/ecount_results.md:349) · [Modular-curve counts](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/geometry_results.md:309)
- **An exact scalar identity — proved by expansion.** With \(a_N=a_pa_q\),
  \[
  M=N+1+(p+q)+a_N-(q+1)a_p-(p+1)a_q.
  \]
  Thus even a supplied \(a_N\) leaves the mixed terms. There is also a factor-free *definition* worth distinguishing from an evaluator: \(M\) is the \(N\)-th Dirichlet coefficient of \(\zeta(s)\zeta(s-1)/L(E,s)\). At each good prime \(r\) the first coefficient is \(1+r-a_r\), and coefficients multiply at coprime indices. Fast extraction at a binary squarefree index would solve the problem. Euler-product evaluation by visiting \(p,q\) presupposes the answer. [Modular cross terms](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nativemod_results.md:77)
- **CM and special reduction — known on promises.** At an ordinary split CM prime, \(A_r=\operatorname{Norm}(1-\pi_r)\). One needs the local norm-\(r\) element with the unit or twist choice appropriate to \(E\). A representation of \(N\) as a norm does not provide the two shifted local norms. At supersingular primes \(r>3\), \(a_r=0\). If both primes are supersingular, \(M=N+1+p+q\), so the count still contains the factor sum. At an anomalous prime \(a_r=1\) and \(A_r=r\). These identities simplify promised inputs; a fixed curve does not impose those promises on arbitrary hidden primes. [CM and trace cases](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md:218)

## E3. What partial information buys

**Proved.** For \(E:y^2=f(x)\), let \(b_r\) be the number of roots of the cubic modulo \(r\). Then \(M\) is odd exactly when \(b_p=b_q=0\). The cubic has \(b_pb_q\) roots modulo \(N\), whereas \(\#E[2](\mathbb Z/N)=(1+b_p)(1+b_q)\). Mixed points with one component at infinity explain the difference. For an \(S_3\) cubic, \((\operatorname{disc}f/N)=-1\) forces \(M\) even. The other sign generally leaves ambiguity. At \(\ell=3\), roots of \(\psi_3\) record stable lines, including lines on which Frobenius negates a generator. A rational point of order \(\ell\) over \(\mathbb Q\) makes \(M\bmod\ell=0\) at good primes away from \(\ell\). A general efficient exact evaluator even for fixed \(2\) or \(3\) remains open. PT allows a pure evaluator to succeed by splitting. [Partial counts](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/ecount_results.md:377) · [Theorem PT](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/factorbits_results.md:303)

**Proved recovery threshold.** EXT takes the exact integer \(M\); its factorization is unnecessary. Its randomized quintic-extension twist gives a component killed by \(M\) and, with constant probability, another that survives. For \(q/p<4/3\), Hasse gives \(|M-N|\le W=4.1N^{3/4}+O(\sqrt N)\). Residues with product \(D>2W\) uniquely recover \(M\). This costs about **three quarters of the input bits** in combined modulus, or auxiliary primes up to about \(0.52\log_2N\). A polynomial-size list of lifts also suffices by trying candidates and verifying factors. A modulus of order \(N^{1/4}\) alone leaves order \(N^{1/2}\) candidates in this interval. The bound is sufficient; the repo proves no necessary threshold for other decoders. Without balance, \(M<16N\) gives a simple uniform CRT cutoff. [CRT bound](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/ecount_results.md:367) · [Theorem EXT](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md:467)

## E4. Preregistered experiment

**Measured.** I fixed \(E:y^2=x^3-x+1\) and drew 2,400 semiprimes at each of 40, 60 and 80 bits, with no reused primes within a size. Four \(N\)-only features took the Jacobi symbol of the norm of \(x^N-x+c\), for \(c=0,1\), in the cubic and 3-division algebras. PARI prime-field counts supplied truth. All 14,400 primes were certified. The baseline conditioned on bit length, \(N\bmod24\), and \((-23/N)\). [Preregistration](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/inp_c/ecount_diga_prereg.txt) · [Runnable experiment](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/inp_c/ecount_diga.py)

Across twelve registered feature–target comparisons, the largest absolute within-stratum correlation was **0.0155**, with family-wise permutation **\(p=0.886\)**. The planted control reached **0.266** against a 95% null cutoff of **0.0329**. There were zero proper gcd splits and zero violations of the forced-even rule. This rules out a detectable association for these four readouts at this sample size. Nonlinear combinations and much weaker signals remain untested. [Exact results](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/inp_c/ecount_diga_result.json)

## E5. Verdict

**Open subproblem.** For the fixed experimental curve, compute the \(N\)-th coefficient of \(\zeta(s)\zeta(s-1)/L(E,s)\) modulo a supplied good prime \(\ell\le 2\ln N\), uniformly in polynomial time from \((N,\ell)\), without factoring \(N\) or receiving local Frobenius data. The product of these primes eventually exceeds \(16N\), so this one scalar algorithm gives the full native count on every good semiprime.

**Speculative probability.** Conditional on classical polynomial-time factoring existing, I now put **about 5%** on native fixed-curve counting being the route to its discovery, down from 24%. The earlier weight rewarded the strength of EXT. Looking inside the evaluator changes my assessment: every established method uses the local characteristic at an identifiable step, and even the first nonconstant residues lack a general native evaluator. The experimental null contributes much less to that revision than the method-by-method obstruction.